    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This study seeks to find if the structure of the demands of the Stanford quarter system impacts the\r\nlevels of depression reported in its student body. That is, can the external and systemic stressors\r\nat Stanford University be linked to levels of depressive symptoms? I propose that depressive\r\nsymptoms may be linked to external stresses exerted on the student in the form of school and\r\nextracurricular time commitments, as well as the occurrence of midterms, exams, and other\r\nmajor points of grading or evaluation. I analyze self-reported time-series data on depressive\r\nsymptoms and external stressors from a random sample of thirty-seven Stanford University\r\nstudents in the spring quarter of the 2008-2009 academic year. Within the limitations of the\r\nstudy, results indicate that depressive symptoms have a strong relationship to the quarterly\r\nstructure and tend to vary not with time constraints but with midterms and major assignments.\r\nDepressive symptoms peak sharply around the time of midterms. Further, Stanford\r\nundergraduates are at baseline remarkably more prone to depressive symptoms than the\r\nnational average. These conclusions suggest that depressive symptoms may be linked to the\r\nstructure of the quarter system due to the condensed occurrences of major assignments,\r\nparticularly when considered in relationship to the campus culture of excellence and\r\nachievement.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Goldring, M., Rosenfeld, M., and Stanford University Sociology Department (2024). Cycling Through the Blues: The Impact of Systemic External Stressors on Student Mental States and Symptoms of Depression. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/nb999zf0622.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
